// sfn/test — host tool-presence probing for tests (SFN-840).
//
// A test that drives an external tool (`tar`, `jq`, `openssl`, `python3`,
// `ld.lld`, `timeout`) has to decide whether that tool exists on the host
// before it can either use it or skip the leg that needs it. The idiom the
// test tree reached for was a shell:
//
//     process.run(["sh", "-c", "command " + "-v " + name])
//
// That spawns a POSIX shell purely to perform a `PATH` lookup, so it fails
// outright on a native Windows host — the reason SFN-646's de-shell
// inventory and this capsule's own migration care about it. It is also the
// shape `.claude/rules/no-bash-e2e.md` is aimed at: the rule bans bash *test
// files*, and the observed adaptation was to keep the bash and move it
// inside a string literal.
//
// `tool_present` is the shell-free replacement, hoisted here so the fifteen
// e2e files that each carried a private `_has_cmd` copy share one
// implementation. It runs the tool directly and reads the exit code.
//
// WHY THE TEST IS "did it spawn", NOT "did it exit 0". `command -v` reports
// whether the binary resolved on `PATH`; it never ran anything, so it
// succeeds for an installed tool regardless of what that tool does with
// `--version`. An `exit == 0` probe is therefore *stricter* than the
// `command -v` it stands in for: it reports a tool as absent when the tool
// is installed but rejects `--version` (`openssl` before 3.0 answers
// `--version` with an error), silently disabling coverage on such a host —
// the same failure mode `docs/conventions/e2e-tests.md` records for the old
// `command -v timeout` probe.
//
// `-1` IS THE WHOLE ABSENCE TEST, and the reason is specific to the wrapper
// this calls. `process.run_capture` routes to `_run_capture_impl` in
// `runtime/sfn/process.sfn`, and *every* failure path there returns `-1`:
// empty argv, argv/envp marshalling, pipe/malloc/file-actions setup,
// `posix_spawnp` reporting `ENOENT` for a binary that is not on `PATH`, and
// a failed `wait`. It never synthesizes `127`. (`process.run` — a different
// function, which this does not call — does return `127` on spawn failure,
// the conventional shell "could not execute" code. Do not carry that
// constant over here: on the `run_capture` path a `127` can only be a
// genuine child exit code, so treating it as absence would misreport a
// present tool that happens to exit 127 — e.g. a `PATH` wrapper script
// ending in `exec <inner-tool>` where the inner tool is missing.)
//
// Anything `>= 0` therefore means the binary resolved and executed, which is
// what `command -v` reports.
//
// KNOWN SLACK, documented rather than fixed: `-1` conflates "not on `PATH`"
// with "the runtime could not spawn at all" (`EMFILE`/`ENOMEM`, plausible
// under `--jobs N`). A resource exhaustion therefore reads as absence and
// becomes a silent skip rather than a loud failure. Callers use this to skip
// a leg, never to fail one, so the blast radius is a missed test rather than
// a wrong result.
//
// The probe inherits the parent environment via `process.environ()`. Note
// this is NOT what resolves the binary: `_run_capture_impl` hands `argv[0]`
// to `posix_spawnp`, which resolves against the *calling* process's `PATH`
// via `getenv`, independent of the `envp` passed to the child — a probe run
// with an empty env still resolves. The parent env is passed because the
// probed tool may itself need `HOME`/`TMPDIR`/locale to run at all, and
// because it matches the `process.run` calls this replaces, which passed the
// parent `environ` through unconditionally. This is not the nested-runner
// case that `clean_runner_env` exists for — the probe child is
// `<tool> --version`, not a nested `sfn test`/`build`/`run`, so it carries
// none of the pool-key leak hazard described in
// `docs/conventions/e2e-tests.md`.
// True when `tool` resolves on the host's `PATH` and can be executed.
//
// Mirrors `command -v <tool>`: it reports presence, not usability. A tool
// that runs but rejects `--version` is present. Callers use this to *skip* a
// leg that needs the tool, never to fail one.
//
// Unlike `command -v`, this actually *executes* the tool, and
// `process.run_capture` leaves the child's stdin inherited and applies no
// deadline. Only probe tools whose `--version` is non-interactive and
// terminates on its own; a tool that reads stdin would hang the pool.
fn tool_present(tool: string) -> boolean ![io] {
    let exit = process.run_capture([tool, "--version"], process.environ());
    let _out = process.capture_take_stdout();
    let _err = process.capture_take_stderr();
    // Negative is the runtime's "the call could not complete" signal, of
    // which a binary that is not on `PATH` is the overwhelmingly common
    // case. Every other code means the tool ran.
    return exit >= 0;
}

// First present tool from `candidates`, or `""` when none resolve.
//
// The `timeout` / `gtimeout` pair is the recurring case: coreutils installs
// `timeout` on Linux and Homebrew installs it as `gtimeout` on macOS, so a
// caller wants "whichever of these exists". Returning the name rather than a
// boolean lets the caller use it as an argv element.
fn first_present_tool(candidates: string[]) -> string ![io] {
    let mut i = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= candidates.length { break; }
        let candidate = candidates[i];
        if tool_present(candidate) { return candidate; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return "";
}
